1
00:00:02,213 --> 00:00:03,866
Now let's talk about the queue time

2
00:00:03,866 --> 00:00:06,015
complexity and compare that to arrays.

3
00:00:06,432 --> 00:00:08,485
Again, I'm comparing queues to arrays here

4
00:00:08,534 --> 00:00:10,427
because both are list structures.

5
00:00:11,405 --> 00:00:12,833
What about accessing elements?

6
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:16,923
Well, for queues, that's O, constant time,

7
00:00:16,955 --> 00:00:19,153
but of course we can only access the, the

8
00:00:19,185 --> 00:00:22,105
first element. So we can basically just

9
00:00:22,329 --> 00:00:24,495
dequeue an item and that gives us access to

10
00:00:24,495 --> 00:00:26,179
that item and that's always the first

11
00:00:26,243 --> 00:00:28,345
element we added. It's the oldest element in

12
00:00:28,345 --> 00:00:28,650
the queue.

13
00:00:29,373 --> 00:00:31,009
For arrays, with the index, we are more

14
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:33,320
flexible. We can access any item, and if we

15
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,631
have the index, we can do that with constant

16
00:00:35,631 --> 00:00:35,824
time.

17
00:00:36,771 --> 00:00:38,071
What about insertion at the end?

18
00:00:38,761 --> 00:00:41,344
Well, for stacks, this was no problem. For

19
00:00:41,425 --> 00:00:43,912
queues, we can only add something new at the

20
00:00:43,912 --> 00:00:45,003
beginning of the queue,

21
00:00:45,613 --> 00:00:47,684
so which is then technically the last

22
00:00:47,684 --> 00:00:50,331
element in the sense of it needs to wait the

23
00:00:50,396 --> 00:00:53,124
longest, but it's the latest element we

24
00:00:53,204 --> 00:00:55,532
added. And therefore this is only added at

25
00:00:55,532 --> 00:00:56,078
the beginning.

26
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,091
So if we really wanna add an item at the end

27
00:01:00,348 --> 00:01:03,302
of the queue so that it's processed next,

28
00:01:03,912 --> 00:01:05,068
if that's what we wanna do,

29
00:01:05,743 --> 00:01:07,910
then we have to remove all other elements

30
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:09,820
first and then we can add the item. And

31
00:01:09,836 --> 00:01:12,341
therefore it's O because we need to go

32
00:01:12,389 --> 00:01:15,022
through the entire queue and empty it so

33
00:01:15,054 --> 00:01:17,382
that we can add a new item that is then the,

34
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:19,549
the last item, so the next item to be

35
00:01:19,549 --> 00:01:20,095
processed.

36
00:01:20,621 --> 00:01:23,521
For arrays, it's O because if we add

37
00:01:23,554 --> 00:01:24,899
something at the end, we can do this with

38
00:01:24,931 --> 00:01:27,021
push, and that's quite efficient. It's

39
00:01:27,038 --> 00:01:27,734
constant time.

40
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:30,220
If you want to insert something at the

41
00:01:30,236 --> 00:01:33,144
beginning, and beginning simply means that

42
00:01:33,273 --> 00:01:35,780
this item now needs to wait until the other

43
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:38,592
items are processed, then this is O in the

44
00:01:38,592 --> 00:01:38,883
queue,

45
00:01:39,417 --> 00:01:41,149
of course, because that's what the queue is

46
00:01:41,262 --> 00:01:43,576
built for. We can enqueue a new item, and

47
00:01:43,625 --> 00:01:43,803
now

48
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:46,854
that happens instantly. And is very

49
00:01:46,854 --> 00:01:47,223
efficient.

50
00:01:48,121 --> 00:01:50,013
For arrays, and that's a difference now,

51
00:01:50,462 --> 00:01:53,157
that's actually O because inserting

52
00:01:53,189 --> 00:01:54,199
something at the beginning

53
00:01:54,776 --> 00:01:58,449
of an array, that is quite inefficient in

54
00:01:58,481 --> 00:02:01,768
quotes because all other array items need to

55
00:02:01,768 --> 00:02:04,575
be shifted to the right then because they

56
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:05,778
get a new index.

57
00:02:06,355 --> 00:02:08,761
Side note, this is also something we talked

58
00:02:08,761 --> 00:02:11,134
about when we compared the linked list to

59
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:13,683
arrays. For linked lists, adding something

60
00:02:13,731 --> 00:02:16,055
at the beginning is very efficient. For

61
00:02:16,136 --> 00:02:18,572
arrays, it's not. This could be one reason

62
00:02:18,588 --> 00:02:21,201
why you wanna use a linked list and it's now

63
00:02:21,329 --> 00:02:22,676
the same for queues.

64
00:02:23,477 --> 00:02:25,224
Though of course, it is worth mentioning

65
00:02:25,256 --> 00:02:27,276
that this depends on how you build your

66
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:28,302
queue internally.

67
00:02:28,943 --> 00:02:31,011
And that's important because for the stack,

68
00:02:31,107 --> 00:02:33,351
it didn't make a difference if we built it

69
00:02:33,351 --> 00:02:35,451
with an array or with the linked list

70
00:02:35,451 --> 00:02:35,964
internally.

71
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:38,609
For the queue, that does make a difference.

72
00:02:39,186 --> 00:02:39,893
If our queue

73
00:02:40,470 --> 00:02:43,214
uses the normal array internally, like this

74
00:02:43,279 --> 00:02:44,193
array version does,

75
00:02:44,819 --> 00:02:47,515
then enqueueing of course means that we call

76
00:02:47,627 --> 00:02:50,725
the regular unshift method, and this method

77
00:02:50,821 --> 00:02:54,030
is O because it will go through all the

78
00:02:54,111 --> 00:02:55,715
items in the array and shift them to the

79
00:02:55,715 --> 00:02:58,684
right. So therefore, this queue using the

80
00:02:58,700 --> 00:02:59,502
internal array

81
00:03:00,048 --> 00:03:03,001
is not the most optimal version because for

82
00:03:03,177 --> 00:03:06,692
enqueueing, which should be O, This queue,

83
00:03:07,045 --> 00:03:08,940
because it uses the array internally,

84
00:03:09,438 --> 00:03:10,595
actually has O.

85
00:03:11,365 --> 00:03:13,421
Here the linked list version is better

86
00:03:13,694 --> 00:03:15,685
because for the linked list, adding

87
00:03:15,718 --> 00:03:18,094
something at the end or at the beginning is

88
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:22,430
really O. And therefore here the linked list

89
00:03:22,687 --> 00:03:25,241
is actually better. The linked list version

90
00:03:25,337 --> 00:03:28,228
of the queue is actually better than the

91
00:03:28,228 --> 00:03:30,380
array-based version. So here it really

92
00:03:30,396 --> 00:03:33,382
matters how you build this internally. Which

93
00:03:33,382 --> 00:03:34,778
is why I'm emphasizing it here.

94
00:03:35,869 --> 00:03:37,907
What about insertion in the middle? Well,

95
00:03:38,131 --> 00:03:40,329
it's not really possible. We would have to

96
00:03:40,442 --> 00:03:43,057
dequeue all items until we reach the point

97
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:44,998
where we wanna add the new item, and

98
00:03:44,998 --> 00:03:47,228
therefore this comes with data loss and is

99
00:03:47,405 --> 00:03:50,999
O. And for arrays, it's also O. And it

100
00:03:51,031 --> 00:03:52,860
doesn't matter how we build our queue

101
00:03:52,876 --> 00:03:54,560
internally, this is always true.

102
00:03:55,282 --> 00:03:56,967
And what about searching elements? It's

103
00:03:56,967 --> 00:03:59,261
basically the same. We would have to dequeue

104
00:03:59,518 --> 00:04:01,363
all items until we found the element we're

105
00:04:01,443 --> 00:04:03,769
looking for, This comes with data loss and

106
00:04:03,769 --> 00:04:05,839
is O and it's the same for arrays.

107
00:04:06,978 --> 00:04:08,518
So here we really just got this key

108
00:04:08,518 --> 00:04:11,405
difference with the O for insertion at the

109
00:04:11,405 --> 00:04:13,860
beginning if the queue is implemented

110
00:04:13,876 --> 00:04:16,475
correctly, for example, with a linked list.

111
00:04:17,646 --> 00:04:20,565
And that's it for queues and what you should

112
00:04:20,614 --> 00:04:23,982
know about them. Here, unlike for stacks, we

113
00:04:24,063 --> 00:04:26,966
even have situations where we can get a

114
00:04:26,966 --> 00:04:29,758
better performance. Nonetheless, just like

115
00:04:29,758 --> 00:04:32,629
stacks, the main application area of queues

116
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:35,525
probably is that you want to force a certain

117
00:04:35,541 --> 00:04:38,277
behavior and you want to restrict the

118
00:04:38,438 --> 00:04:40,916
options other developers have when working

119
00:04:40,932 --> 00:04:43,362
with that data structure or do anything like

120
00:04:43,442 --> 00:04:43,699
that.
